{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"static-members","title":"Static Members","group":"Classes & Objects","order":30,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <code>static</code> member belongs to the class itself, not to any object: one shared copy of a data member, and member functions callable without an object.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Static data and functions</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nclass User {\npublic:\n    explicit User(std::string name) : name_(std::move(name)), id_(++created_) { ++alive_; }\n    ~User() { --alive_; }\n    User(const User&amp;) = delete;\n\n    static int alive() { return alive_; }       // no `this`: can only touch static members\n    int id() const { return id_; }\n\n    static constexpr int kMaxNameLength = 32;    // compile-time constant\nprivate:\n    std::string name_;\n    int id_;\n    inline static int created_ = 0;              // C++17: define in-class with `inline`\n    static int alive_;                           // classic: declared here…\n};\nint User::alive_ = 0;                            // …defined once in a .cpp\n\nint main() {\n    User a(\"ada\");\n    {\n        User b(\"bob\");\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; User::alive() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; b.id() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    }\n    User c(\"cy\");\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; User::alive() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; c.id() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; User::kMaxNameLength &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2 2\n2 3 32</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Non-static member</th><th>Static member</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Belongs to</td><td>Each object</td><td>The class (one copy)</td></tr><tr><td>Access</td><td><code>obj.m</code></td><td><code>Class::m</code> (or <code>obj.m</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Has <code>this</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>No — can't use non-static members directly</td></tr><tr><td>Can be <code>virtual</code> / <code>const</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-building\"></i>Singleton &amp; factory patterns</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nclass Logger {\npublic:\n    static Logger&amp; instance() {            // Meyers singleton: thread-safe lazy init (C++11)\n        static Logger logger;\n        return logger;\n    }\n    void log(const std::string&amp; msg) { std::cout &lt;&lt; ++count_ &lt;&lt; \": \" &lt;&lt; msg &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\n    Logger(const Logger&amp;) = delete;\n    Logger&amp; operator=(const Logger&amp;) = delete;\nprivate:\n    Logger() = default;                    // nobody else can construct one\n    int count_ = 0;\n};\n\nclass Temperature {\n    double kelvin_;\n    explicit Temperature(double k) : kelvin_(k) {}\npublic:\n    static Temperature fromCelsius(double c) { return Temperature(c + 273.15); }   // named constructors\n    static Temperature fromKelvin(double k)  { return Temperature(k); }\n    double kelvin() const { return kelvin_; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Logger::instance().log(\"start\");\n    Logger::instance().log(\"again\");\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; Temperature::fromCelsius(25).kelvin() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1: start\n2: again\n298.15</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>The many meanings of <code>static</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Class member</td><td>Shared by all objects</td></tr><tr><td>Local variable</td><td>Keeps its value between calls; initialised once</td></tr><tr><td>Namespace-scope function/variable</td><td>Internal linkage — private to this <code>.cpp</code> (prefer an anonymous namespace)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Global and static objects in <em>different</em> <code>.cpp</code> files are initialised in an unspecified order (the <em>static initialisation order fiasco</em>). If one global's constructor uses another, wrap it in a function returning a function-local static.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why can't a static member function be const or virtual?</summary><div><p>Both are about <code>this</code>: <code>const</code> qualifies the object the function is called on, and virtual dispatch uses the object's vtable. A static member function has no object and no <code>this</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}